### docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-08-tui-project-management-design.md
+1: # TUI Project Management Design
+2: 
+3: ## Context
+4: 
+5: `docs/isusses/tui-project-management.md` defines a new TUI product requirement: users should be able to open a new filesystem directory as a project and switch between existing projects from inside the TUI. This is separate from workspace/worktree management. `/move` and `/warp` stay scoped to the current project boundary and do not satisfy this requirement.
+6: 
+7: The web app already has project selection and project opening behavior. Its directory picker uses server-side SDK calls to list and search directories. The TUI design should use the web app as a reference only where the TUI-specific decisions below do not already define behavior.
+8: 
+9: ## Goals
+10: 
+11: - Add a TUI project list reachable with `/projects`.
+12: - Let users switch to an existing project without restarting the TUI.
+13: - Let users open a new project directory from the project list.
+14: - Keep project deletion out of scope.
+15: - Support Windows absolute paths.
+16: - Show each project with both a display name and directory.
+17: - Preserve TUI keyboard-first interaction.
+18: - Avoid registering browsed intermediate directories as opened projects.
+19: 
+20: ## Non-Goals
+21: 
+22: - No project deletion flow.
+23: - No Windows-style file explorer UI.
+24: - No direct command for the add-project page.
+25: - No automatic jump to the target project's latest session.
+26: - No broad redesign of workspace/worktree management.
+27: 
+28: ## Existing Code Facts
+29: 
+30: - TUI currently has `workspace.list` and `DialogWorkspaceList`, but that flow manages experimental workspaces and includes deletion. It is not the project management flow required here.
+31: - TUI `SDKProvider` currently receives a startup `directory` and creates an SDK client with that default directory.
+32: - TUI `ProjectProvider` and `SyncProvider` derive project, path, sessions, providers, agents, config, VCS, and related state from the SDK default directory plus the current workspace.
+33: - Backend `Project.Service.list()` returns projects stored in `ProjectTable`; `/project` exposes this as the list of projects opened with OpenCode.
+34: - Backend `Project.fromDirectory(directory)` upserts project state and records known directories.
+35: - Existing `file.list` and `find.files` endpoints use `InstanceContextMiddleware`. That middleware calls `InstanceStore.load({ directory })`, which can call `Project.fromDirectory(directory)`. Therefore those endpoints must not be used for side-effect-free browsing of arbitrary parent directories in this feature.
+36: 
+37: ## Architecture
+38: 
+39: Use a TUI soft re-enter model for project switching.
+40: 
+41: When the user selects an existing project or confirms a new project directory:
+42: 
+43: - Update the TUI active project directory.
+44: - Recreate the SDK default directory or remount the project-scoped provider subtree with the target directory.
+45: - Clear the current dialog.
+46: - Navigate to the `home` route.
+47: - Let normal project bootstrap load the target project context.
+48: 
+49: This intentionally behaves like starting the TUI in the target directory, except the process is not restarted. This is preferred over passing `directory` through every SDK call, because explicit per-call routing would be easy to miss and could leave stale project state mixed with the new project.
+50: 
+51: ## Project List Page
+52: 
+53: Entry:
+54: 
+55: - Register a TUI command named `project.list`.
+56: - Use slash name `projects`, so `/projects` opens the project list.
+57: - The add-project page has no independent command entry and can only be opened from this page.
+58: 
+59: Data:
+60: 
+61: - Load existing projects from `sdk.client.project.list()`.
+62: - Do not maintain a separate TUI project history.
+63: - Do not expose project deletion.
+64: 
+65: Display:
+66: 
+67: - Display name priority:
+68:   1. `project.name`, when available.
+69:   2. Basename of `project.worktree`.
+70: - Always show the project directory as secondary detail.
+71: - Mark or highlight the current project.
+72: - Search matches both display name and directory.
+73: 
+74: Keyboard behavior:
+75: 
+76: - Typing filters the project list.
+77: - `up` / `down` moves the selected project.
+78: - `enter` switches to the selected project and soft re-enters its home route.
+79: - `ctrl+o` opens the add-project page.
+80: - `esc` closes the dialog.
+81: 
+82: ## Add Project Page
+83: 
+84: Entry and exit:
+85: 
+86: - The page is only reachable from project list via `ctrl+o`.
+87: - `esc` returns to project list.
+88: - It does not register a command or slash command.
+89: 
+90: Initial state:
+91: 
+92: - The input starts with the parent directory of the current project.
+93: - The selector shows direct child directories under that input directory.
+94: - Example: if the default input is `D:/develop/opencode`, the selector shows entries such as `D:/develop/opencode/subA` and `D:/develop/opencode/subB`.
+95: 
+96: Input and selector semantics:
+97: 
+98: - The input is the source of truth for the project directory that `enter` confirms.
+99: - The selector offers fuzzy-matched direct child directories for the current input context.
+100: - Search filtering uses a `10ms` debounce.
+101: - Typing appends or edits the path in the input.
+102: - Example flow:
+103:   - Initial input: `D:/develop/opencode`.
+104:   - Initial selector: `D:/develop/opencode/subA`, `D:/develop/opencode/subB`, and other direct children.
+105:   - User types `A`.
+106:   - Input becomes `D:/develop/opencode/A`.
+107:   - Selector shows fuzzy matches such as `D:/develop/opencode/subA`.
+108:   - User presses `space`.
+109:   - Input becomes the selected directory, for example `D:/develop/opencode/subA`.
+110:   - Selector reloads direct children of `D:/develop/opencode/subA`.
+111:   - User presses `enter`.
+112:   - The current input value is used as the project directory.
+113: 
+114: Keyboard behavior:
+115: 
+116: - Typing edits the input and filters the current selector layer.
+117: - `up` / `down` moves the selected selector item.
+118: - `space` replaces the input with the selected selector item and loads that directory's direct children.
+119: - `enter` validates and confirms the input value as the project directory. It does not use the selected item unless `space` already copied that item into the input.
+120: - `esc` returns to project list.
+121: 
+122: Path handling:
+123: 
+124: - Support Windows absolute path forms, including drive-letter paths such as `D:/repo` or `D:\repo`.
+125: - Preserve the user's path intent in the TUI input, but normalize enough for comparison, search, and display.
+126: - Server validation resolves the final input path from the server's filesystem perspective.
+127: - The web app picker path helpers may be referenced for missing edge cases, but the confirmed TUI interaction model above has priority.
+128: 
+129: ## Directory Browse API
+130: 
+131: Add a side-effect-free server-side directory browsing API for this feature.
+132: 
+133: Requirements:
+134: 
+135: - Accept a server-side absolute directory path.
+136: - Return direct child directories with name and absolute path.
+137: - Optionally accept a query string or let the TUI filter locally; local TUI filtering is preferred for the current direct-child list.
+138: - Do not use `InstanceContextMiddleware`.
+139: - Do not call `InstanceStore.load`.
+140: - Do not call `Project.fromDirectory`.
+141: - Do not create, update, or touch project rows.
+142: - Do not recursively scan.
+143: - Keep authorization consistent with the existing HTTP API surface.
+144: 
+145: Error behavior:
+146: 
+147: - If the path does not exist, is not a directory, or cannot be read, return a structured error suitable for a toast.
+148: - The TUI remains on the add-project page after browse errors.
+149: - A failed child-directory read does not change the current input.
+150: 
+151: This API exists because the current `file.list` and `find.files` endpoints are not side-effect-free for arbitrary directory browsing.
+152: 
+153: ## Confirming A Project Directory
+154: 
+155: When `enter` is pressed in the add-project page:
+156: 
+157: - Use the current input value as the project directory.
+158: - Validate that the path resolves to an existing directory on the server.
+159: - Trigger the TUI soft re-enter flow for that directory.
+160: - The normal project bootstrap may then call `Project.fromDirectory`, which is the correct moment to create or update the project record.
+161: - Navigate to target project home.
+162: 
+163: ## Error Handling
+164: 
+165: - Existing project switch failure: stay on project list and show an error toast.
+166: - Directory browse failure: stay on add-project page and show an error toast.
+167: - Confirmed input is not a valid directory: stay on add-project page and show an error toast.
+168: - Soft re-enter bootstrap failure: surface the normal TUI bootstrap error behavior for the target directory.
+169: 
+170: ## Testing Requirements
+171: 
+172: - `/projects` opens the project list.
+173: - Project list loads from backend `/project`.
+174: - Project list search matches display name and directory.
+175: - Current project is marked or highlighted.
+176: - Project list does not show deletion actions.
+177: - `ctrl+o` opens the add-project page from project list.
+178: - Add-project page cannot be opened directly by a command.
+179: - `esc` from add-project page returns to project list.
+180: - Add-project initial input is the current project parent directory.
+181: - Add-project selector lists only direct child directories.
+182: - Add-project input filtering uses `10ms` debounce.
+183: - `up` / `down` move selected items in both pages.
+184: - `space` in add-project page copies the selected directory into the input and loads its direct children.
+185: - `enter` in add-project page confirms the input value, not the selected item.
+186: - Confirming a directory soft re-enters the target project home.
+187: - Directory browse API does not call `Project.fromDirectory` and does not add browsed directories to `/project`.
+188: - Windows absolute paths are preserved through the TUI input and parsed by the server-side validation path.
+189: 
+190: ## Implementation Boundaries
+191: 
+192: - Keep changes focused on TUI project management and the minimal backend API needed for side-effect-free directory browsing.
+193: - Do not change workspace deletion, project copy deletion, `/move`, or `/warp` behavior.
+194: - Do not edit generated SDK files directly. If the public Protocol or Server `HttpApi` changes, run the repository generation command from `packages/client` during implementation planning/execution.
+195: - Do not commit unless explicitly requested.

